## A B S T R A C T
For treatment of wound infection with stabilized iodine, potassium iodide cyclodextrin metal-organic frame­ works (KI-CD-MOF) was prepared to carry iodine via gas-solid reaction. Apart from highly ordered porous frameworks, KI-CD-MOF contains uniformly distributed iodide ions which stabilize iodine $\left(\mathrm{I}_{2}\right)$ . X-ray photo­ electron spectroscopy and potentiometric titration were utilized to confirm the formation of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ in the highly porous KI-CD-MOF as $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF. Molecular simulation and characterizations of the synchrotron radiation Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy, differential scanning calorimeter, powder X-ray diffraction and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption were conducted to illustrate the inclusion mechanism of iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}.$ CD-MOF. The apparent solubility of iodine in water was 3.86 times enhanced. The stability and antibacterial activity tests demonstrated that the highly-dispersed iodide ions in KI-CD-MOF are crucial in improvement to the solubility, stability, and bacteriostatic effects of active iodide. Therefore, KI-CD-MOF has broad application prospects and advantages in efficiently capturing and stabilizing iodine.
## 1. Introduction
Wound infection is a significant global health problem that affects human life and health, which prevents the wound from healing and produces sever health effects. To prevent the infection in the wound sites and promote the wound healing, inhibition of bacterial growth is main driving force for development of disinfectant (Vermeulen et al., 2010; Wang & Wang, 2019). Iodine, one of the most recognized chemical agents used in wound healing has broad-spectrum antibacterial activity against bacteria, molds, and certain viruses. The bactericidal mechanism is that iodine can quickly pass through the cell wall, interfere with and destroy the functions of proteins, enzymes, and nucleotides in micro­ organisms, thus leading to microbial physiological, metabolic dysfunction and death (Akhmetova et al., 2016). However, the appli­ cations of iodine have been greatly restricted by low water-solubility and a high degree of chemo-physical instability (Selvaggi et al., 2003). Many “iodophors” (iodine carriers or iodine releasing agents) have been designed, but most of them introduced liquid potassium iodide to improve the solubility and stability of iodine (Klimaviciute et al., 2012; Tang et al., 2015; Zhang et al., 2020). This indicates the preparation process is more cumbersome in terms of the production process if the solvent must be removed to turn it into solid dosage forms. Currently, the most widely used iodine disinfectant products are iodine tincture and iodine complexes incorporating solubilizers or carriers (povidoneiodine). Iodine tincture is an irritating and volatile alcoholic solution of iodine (Johnson et al., 2015). However, it cannot be used for disinfection of mucosa owing to its irritation. Although povidone-iodine overcomes the problem of iodine tincture, it still introduces liquid po­ tassium iodide (Herruzo et al., 2018). Therefore, developing a novel “iodophor” is still a challenging but meaningful task for achieving costeffective disinfection.
Cyclodextrin metal-organic framework (CD-MOF) is an extended and ordered network porous crystal material with cyclodextrin as organic ligand and alkali metal ions (e.g., $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ and $\mathbf{Rb}^{+}$ ) as inorganic coupling units. These crystal cubes have high specific surface area, sizeable adjustable cavity, and can load and adsorb guest molecules into its unique nano-meter sized molecular cavities to improve the stability of guest molecules via molecular encapsulation (Li, Lv, et al., 2017; Li, Guo, et al., 2017; Lv et al., 2017; Moussa et al., 2016; Zhang et al., 2018). At the same time, it is used to enhance the solubility and bioavailability of drugs (Chen et al., 2021). Due to its potential biomedical usages, CDMOF attracts plenty of attention and develops rapidly for its function­ alities and applications. To date, impregnation and co-crystallization remain the most popular method for drug loading into CD-MOF in so­ lution, while grinding for the solid-phase operation is also reported (Han, Liu, et al., 2018). For instance, sucralose was successfully encapsulated into basic or neutralized CD-MOF by impregnation to significantly improve thermal stability at elevated temperatures (Lv et al., 2017). Grinding is another efficient way to load drugs in the solid phase, but it dissociates the structure of CD-MOF and reduces the drug release profile (Zhang, Guo, et al., 2019). The co-crystallization method is also utilized to load poorly soluble drugs into CD-MOF, such as leflunomide (Kritskiy et al., 2019), methotrexate (Kritskiy et al., 2020), ibuprofen (Li, Lv, et al., 2017), and lansoprazole (Li, Guo, et al., 2017). To achieve effective therapy for periodontitis, our recent study applied a cross-linked CD-MOF to carry iodine by impregnation (Lu et al., 2021). However, this strategy of loading iodine introduced organic solvents and had a low utilization rate of iodine. For the design of the external dosage form of iodine powder, the volatilization of iodine will be caused in the process of removing the solvent as a very thorny challenge. CD-MOF is capable of effective adsorbing gas. For example, it was reported to absorb HCHO effectively with high selectivity, high speed, and high capacity (Wang et al., 2018), and $S\mathrm{F}_{6}$ was embedded into CD-MOF via a nonchemical adsorptive process (Han, Guo, et al., 2018). It is widely known that iodine has a property of sublimation. Therefore, it is ex­ pected to give birth to new sparkles for treatment of wound infection when iodine is carried by CD-MOF in the form of gas without intro­ ducing any solvent. Since CD-MOF dissolves instantly into free $\gamma$ -CD when it is applied to wound or skin and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ is of injectable grade, it is generally regarded as safe for CD-MOF to be applied to wounds or skin. Similarly, In vivo wound healing experiments based on GS5-CL-Ag@CDMOF, a system made of CD-MOF, have been done by our colleagues, and the results showed that it was conducive to rapid wound healing by exerting hemostasis and maintenance of microbial free environment at the wound site, and no allergy was found in the wound of rats during the experiment (Shakya et al., 2019).
In the study, KI-CD-MOF was prepared as carrier for iodine to avoid problems caused by the removal of solvent. It is especially of interest that $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF contained highly-dispersed coordinate iodide ions if the iodide ions capture evaporated iodine stably via gas-solid reaction. It was speculated that the mechanism of the capture of iodine was the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ and cyclodextrin molecular cavity encapsulation, which was verified by the determination of potentiometric titration and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The potential of KI-CD-MOF as a carrier for iodine in the treatment of wound infection was evaluated by solubility, stability, and bactericidal activity study.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Pharmaceutical grade crystalline iodine $\left(\mathrm{I}_{2}\right)$ was provided by
Shandong Jiejing Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd. (China). The $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was ob­ tained from Maxdragon (G. Z.) Biochem Ltd. (China). Cydiodine was purchased from Huasu (B. J.) Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd. (China). Iodine tincture was purchased from Heng Jian (G. Z.) Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd. Yeast extract and tryptone were purchased from Thermo Fisher Scien­ tific Ltd. (USA). Sodium chloride (NaCl), agarose, sodium cyanite $\mathrm{(NaHSO_{3})}$ , ammonium thiocyanate $\mathrm{(NH}_{4}S{\mathrm{CN}})$ , silver nitrate $\left({\tt A g N O}_{3}\right)$ , potassium acetate $\mathrm{\cdot{CH_{3}C O O K)}}$ , potassium iodide (KI), ethanol (EtOH), and other reagents were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China) and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of CH3COOK-CD-MOF and KI-CD-MOF
Owing to that $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ is highly symmetric and possesses the largest cavity in contrast to $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\alpha-CD}$ and $\upbeta$ -CD, $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD-MOF provides the most attractive prospect for drug loading, while $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ is much safe and highly soluble in water. Therefore, $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was chosen as the organic ligand to synthesize CD-MOF in this study. Potassium acetate $_{(3.03\mathrm{~g)~}}$ and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ $_{(20.00\ g)}$ were dissolved and reacted in deionized water $\mathrm{20~mL}$ at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{:}$ , then transferred and cooled at room temperature (RT) (Ding et al., 2019). When a small number of crystals precipitated, $80~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous ethanol was added. After $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at RT, the reaction mixture was centrifuged and washed twice with $160~\mathrm{\mL}$ anhydrous ethanol to purification. $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF was dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For the synthesis of KI-CD-MOF, similar process was followed by using potas­ sium iodide $_{(5.12\ \mathrm{g})}$ instead of potassium acetate, and the dosage of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was $14.25~\mathrm{g}$ . The purities of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF and KI-CD-MOF were about $98\%$ calculated by the combined $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ using a method developed by the co-authors.
## 2.3. Loading of iodine into CD-MOF, $\gamma{-}C D$
The dried KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ powder $_{(5.00\mathrm{g})}$ were precisely weighed into EP tubes, then $2.14\ {\mathrm g}$ of iodine (material ratio of iodine was $30\%$ ) were added to prepare samples $(\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI-CD}.$ MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF, $\operatorname{I}_{2}\@parrow$ -CD) at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . The loading efficiency of KI-CD-MOF was further optimized with different material ratios of iodine from $1\%$ to $40\%$ . In our investigation, the iodine material ratio of $30\%$ and even $1\%$ was obviously overloaded for $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ . In light of that, the material ratio of $1\%$ for iodine loading by $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was adopted as a control in the following drug loading mechanism and antibacterial activity studies.
Colorless, cubic and single crystals of KOH-CD-MOF with similar structure to KI-CD-MOF and $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF were prepared by the methanol vapor diffusion method (Smaldone et al., 2010) to further demonstrate the adsorption process of iodine. The single-crystal parti­ cles and iodine were placed in an airtight container for loading effi­ ciency at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The single-crystal particles were taken out and cut from the middle at 0, 2, 6, 10, 12, and $15\mathrm{min}$ . The colors of the cross-section were observed and photographed.
2.4. Quantification of iodine and iodine ion loaded in $I_{2}@C D$ -MOF and $I_{2}@\gamma\ –C D$
The contents of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ were determined by potentiometric titra­ tion. Briefly, to determine the $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ content, $15\mathrm{mg}$ sample was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ potassium iodide aqueous solution in titration cup and titrated with sodium thiosulfate standard titrant $(0.005\mathrm{\mmol/L})$ through the Automatic Potentiometric Titrator (ZDJ-4B, China) (Eq. (1)). The po­ tential jump point indicated the endpoint of redox titration, and the consumed volume of sodium thiosulfate titrant at the endpoint was recorded. The effective iodine content in the sample was calculated according to the Eq. (2).
$$
\mathrm{I}_{2}+2\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}=\mathrm{S}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}+2\mathrm{I}^{-}
$$
$$
\mathrm{{m}(I_{2},\mathrm{{mg})=\frac{C\left(N a_{2}S_{2}O_{3}\right)\times V\left(N a_{2}S_{2}O_{3}\right)\times253.809\times10^{-3}}{2}}}
$$
where, $\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{Na_{2}S_{2}O_{3}})$ is the concentration $\mathrm{{(mmol/L)}}$ of the sodium thio­ sulfate titrant, $\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ is the volume (mL) of consumed sodium thiosulfate titrant, and 253.809 is the relative molecular mass $\mathbf{(g/mol)}$ of iodine.
Similarly, iodine content was determined by dissolving $35~\mathrm{mg}$ of the sample into $40\mathrm{mL}$ sodium bisulfite solution $\mathrm{(0.024mol/L)}$ . In next step, $4~\mathrm{mL}$ sulfuric acid and $15~\mathrm{mL}$ silver nitrate solution $(0.01\mathrm{mol/L})$ were added and the solution was titrated with ammonium thiocyanate titrant $(0.01\mathrm{mol/L})$ by the automatic potentiometric titrator as per Eqs. (3) and (4). The effective $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ content in the sample was calculated according to Eq. (5).
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}+\mathbf{I}^{-}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\downarrow}}\\ &{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}+\mathbf{S}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{N}^{-}=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{\downarrow}}\\ &{\mathbf{m}(\mathbf{I}^{-},\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g})=[15\times\mathbf{C}\left(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{O}_{3}\right)-\mathbf{C}\left(\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{N}\right)\times\mathbf{V}\left(\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{4}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{N}\right)]\times126.9}\end{array}
$$
where $\mathsf{C}(\mathsf{A g N O}_{3})$ and $\mathsf{C}(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathsf{S C N})$ are the concentrations $\mathrm{{(mmol/L)}}$ of the silver nitrate solution and ammonium thiocyanate titrant, respec­ tively. $\mathrm{V(NH}_{4}S{\mathrm{CN}})$ is the volume (mL) of consumed ammonium thio­ cyanate titrant, and 126.9 is the relative molecular mass $\mathbf{\left(g/mol\right)}$ of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$
## 2.5. Molecular docking
The surface chemical composition of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF was determined on an Axis Ultra Hybrid spectrometer (Thermo Scientific, USA), using Al–K α radiation $.1486.8\mathrm{eV}$ , $10\mathrm{mA}$ emission at 150 W). Binding energy was calibrated using the C 1s photoelectron peak from adventitious hydrocarbon $(284.8\mathrm{eV})$ .
The single crystal structure of KOH-CD-MOF was extracted from the literature (Forgan et al., 2012). The molecular structure of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ was con­ structed manually, and the crystal structure of KI-CD-MOF was con­ structed based on KOH-CD-MOF. The molecular minimization and dynamics simulations were performed using the parallel package of NAMD 2.6 (Phillips et al., 2020) with the general amber forcefield (gaff). The AutoDock Vina 1.1.2 (Schneider et al., 1998) was used to obtain the location of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in KI-CD-MOF. The Gaussian 16 package was used to calculate the partial atomic charges and dimensions of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ for con­ structing the forcefield manually. The forcefield of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was extracted from leaprc. GLYCAM_06h-1 in AmberTools12. A time step of 0.5 fs was used to integrate the equations of motion. The temperature was main­ tained at $310~\mathrm{K},$ employing Langevin dynamics with a damping coeffi­ cient of $1\ {\mathsf{p s}}^{-1}$ . First, the energy minimization was performed; then, a molecular dynamic of 20,000 steps was run. Finally, the energy mini­ mization was performed again. In all processes, all atoms are fixed except I and H atoms.
## 2.6. Morphology and other characterizations
Morphological structure of MOF was captured by field emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, SU7000, Hitachi). The synchrotron radiation Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (SR-FTIR) data were obtained using a spectrometer (Nicolet 6700) at the Shanghai Syn­ chrotron Radiation Facility (SSRF) with a range of $400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The DSC curves were measured with the differential scanning calorimeter (DSC, Q2000 V24.11 Build 124) at the heating rates of $10\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ within the temperature range $25\mathrm{-}300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The thermal behaviors were studied by weighing samples in an aluminum pan under a constant nitrogen gas flow of $50~\mathrm{mL/min}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, D8 Bruker) was used to characterize the crystallinity of the samples. The nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm was carried out using a porosimeter (Micromeritics), and the BET surface areas were obtained from the linear region of $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ isotherms (Walton & Snurr, 2007). The thermogra­ vimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted on thermal analysis system (TGA, Perkin-Elmer Pyris, USA) at a heating rate of $10.00\ {^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min}}$ under a constant nitrogen flow of $20~\mathrm{{mL/min}}$ . The sample (about $5~{\mathrm{mg}}$ ) was loaded into an aluminum pan and the weight loss $(\%)$ of the sample was measured over the temperature from 30 to $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
## 2.7. Solubility studies
The apparent aqueous solubilities of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , $\operatorname{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF, and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\gamma}$ -CD were quantified by the vibration method. Excess amount of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and the iodine loaded samples were dis­ solved with water $\mathrm{~\textmu~mL}$ to prepare the super saturated solution. The solutions were placed into incubation shaker $(200~\mathrm{r/min})$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 h. Subsequently, the solutions were filtered through a $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ mem­ brane filter to eliminate any insoluble particles. The solubility of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in distilled water was measured by potentiometric titration in triplicates.
## 2.8. Stability analysis
About $3.00\ g\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD were placed into weighing bottles in triplicates and exposed to $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , $40\ {}^{\circ}{\bf C},$ $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air dry oven. The residual iodine content in each sample at 0, 2, 4, 8, 12, 24, 48, and $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ was calculated by potentiometric titration $\mathbf{\bar{\boldsymbol{n}}=}$ 3) to check the improved stability.
Three batches of samples were packed and carefully sealed in aluminum foil and placed at $40\pm2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and relative humidity of $75\pm5\%$ for 3 months. Samples were taken at the end of the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd months, respectively. The changes of iodine content were measured and recorded by potentiometric titration $(\mathbf{n}=3)$ ).
## 2.9. Antibacterial activity
The antibacterial activity of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , $\operatorname{I}_{2}({\underline{{\theta}}}$ KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}\cdot\mathrm{Cl}$ DMOF, $\operatorname{I}_{2}@\gamma$ -CD，cydiodine, and iodine tincture were observed by the microdilution method. Sterile Luria Bertani (LB) broth and LB agar media were used to check the antibacterial activity of samples. The concentration of Escherichia coli CMCC (B) 44102 (E. coli) and Staphy­ lococcus aureus CMCC (B) 26112 (S. aureus) was adjusted to $1\times10^{6}$ colony forming units $\mathrm{(CFU)/mL}$ by turbidimetric method $(\mathrm{OD}_{600}=0.1_{.}^{\cdot}$ . The test samples were diluted to 10 consecutive concentrations and added into 1–10 well of 96-well plate $(50~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ . Aliquots of $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ normal saline were added to the 11th and 14th wells, ${\mathsf{50}}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of potassium iodide solution to the 12th and 15th wells, add $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of bacterial suspension to each well of 1–13 wells $(n=3)$ with mixing. The minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) value was the concentration of the hole without turbidity observed by the naked eye after culturing at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After MIC was determined, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ was taken from the MIC well and the three wells above the MIC for the coating to the medium plate and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The concentration of sterile growth was viewed as the minimum bactericidal concentration (MBC) value $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}=3)$ .
The time-sterilization curve is determined according to the method of antibiotics to reflect the effect of killing bacteria at different times and concentrations of iodine-containing samples. The bacteria cultures were shaken at $200\ \mathrm{r/min}$ and preincubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The final concentration of $E$ . coli is $1\times10^{9}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ , and concentrations of iodine were set to $0.25\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , $0.5\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , $1.0\times\mathrm{MIC}$ $2.0\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , and $4.0\times$ MIC, respectively. The final concentration of S. aureus was $1\times10^{7}$ CFU/ mL, and iodine concentrations were set to $0.5\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , $1\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , $2\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , $4\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , and $8\times\mathrm{MIC}.$ respectively. Samples of different concentrations mixed with the bacteria were cultured at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , which were mixed with $5\%$ sodium thiosulfate pentahydrate to stop the bactericidal effect of iodine at $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , 4 h, 6 h and $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. Then the mixture was coated to the solid medium that was count after culturing at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The experiment was repeated 3 times and the average value was calculated.
The sterilization curve was drawn with the action time as the abscissa and the natural logarithm of viable bacteria number as the ordinate. The $\mathbf{CFU/mL}$ was calculated by plate count technique using Eq. (6).
$$
\mathrm{CFU\mL^{-1}={\frac{C o l o n i e s\ n u m b e r\times D i l u t i o n\ r a t i o}{P l a t e d\ v o l u m e}}}
$$
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Molecular mechanisms and simulations of $I_{2}$ loaded by KI-CD-MOF
CD-MOF is a result of coordination bond between of eight $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and six $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ units (Forgan et al., 2012). As a drug carrier, large cavities of CDMOF and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD bimolecular pair provide space drug molecules to stay within. High drug loading and dispersion of iodine can be achieved by using CD-MOF to capture iodine so as to further enhance the bacterio­ static effect, but at the same time, there may be a problem of long-term storage stability. However, KI-CD-MOF can solve this problem due to the high dispersion of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ . Based on spatial structure and drug loading form of CD-MOF, the following speculation about the mechanism of action be­ tween iodine and KI-CD-MOF was put forward: $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ is highly dispersed throughout the cavity of KI-CD-MOF, and evaporated iodine molecule enters the carrier at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , one part of which forms $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ through combining with $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ , the other interacts with the carrier to be wrapped by the mo­ lecular cavity. By potentiometric titration, the contents of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF were determined as $88.9\mathrm{\mg/g}$ and $272.4~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , corre­ sponding to $24.61\%$ and $75.39\%$ of the total iodine, respectively. To determine the content of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ , the iodine in the solution system was reduced to iodide ions with sodium bisulfite firstly, and then the content of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ in $\operatorname{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF was obtained by subtracting the previously measured $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ content from the measured total iodine ion content. The theoretical calculation was carried out according to the molecular mechanism based on the results of potentiometric titration: after the combination of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ $^-\left(88.9\mathrm{mg/g}\right)$ and $\mathrm{I}_{2}\left(177.8\mathrm{mg/g}\right)$ to form $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ that without combining with $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ were $94.6\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $266.7\mathrm{mg/g}$ , which accounted for $26.18\%$ and $73.82\%$ of the total iodine content, respec­ tively. XPS was used to investigate the valence of adsorbed $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}.$ CD-MOF, and two characteristic chemical species $(\mathrm{I}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ at $617.11\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 618.51 eV) were observed by fitting the chemical state of the strong split peak, corresponding to the negative one valence and zero valence of iodine comparing with XPS Handbook, respectively (Fig. 1A) (Zhang, da Silva, et al., 2019). The negative one valence and zero valence of iodine account for $23.10\%$ and $76.90\%$ of the total iodine content using the method of element sensitivity factor, which was consistent with the theoretical value calculated according to the molecular mechanism (Fig. 1B). Hence, the results of XPS cross-validated the speculation, and further calculations showed that almost whole $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ in KI-CD-MOF com­ bined with two-thirds of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ to form $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ .
In view of the fact that the PXRD pattern of KI-CD-MOF powder was similar to that of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF (Ding et al., 2019; Smaldone et al., 2010), it was considered that $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ was involved in the composition of KICD-MOF crystal structure (Fig. 1C, D and E). We fixed all the atoms except hydrogen atom and optimized only the position of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ when obtaining the molecular structure of KI-CD-MOF crystal, and the opti­ mization results (Fig. 1C, D and E) showed the positions of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ in KI-CDMOF observed from different angles. Because the molecular structure of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ was large and linear, it almost filled the whole position of the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ in the KOH-CD-MOF and connected two large cavities from the vertex di­ rection of the cube cavity. After entering KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ molecules preferentially combined with $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ to form $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ linear ions. With the further increase of iodine loading, it was inferred from the results of the mo­ lecular simulation that excess $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ stayed in CD bimolecular pairs. Fig. 1F showed the position of excess $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ molecules in KI-CD-MOF when the material ratio of iodine was $30\%$ . Thus, the molecular simulation results further confirmed that the molecular mechanism of iodine capture by KI-CD-MOF was the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ and cyclodextrin molecular cavity encapsulation.
## 3.2. Characterization and mechanism of I2@KI-CD-MOF
The SR-FTIR (Fig. 2A) showed a characteristic infrared absorption peak of iodine at $1400.31\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . After iodine was loaded by KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ , the characteristic absorption peak of iodine was not evident, which indicated that iodine was well encapsu­ lated. Small shifts were found in the characteristic peaks of KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF and $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD. The characteristic peak of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K\mathrm{-}C D}.$ - MOF shifted from $1028.23~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1026.61~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and that of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD shifted from $1025.98~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1026.60~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while the characteristic peak of KI-CD-MOF shifted from $1027.11~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1025.36~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The shifts suggested that there was certain interaction between iodine and the carrier. Moreover, the highest deviation degree of the characteristic peak is found in KI-CD-MOF. Hence, it is speculated that the interaction between KI-CD-MOF and iodine is stronger due to the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ and cyclodextrin molecular cavity encapsulation.
The DSC thermograph of iodine showed an intrinsic endothermic peak at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and two small exothermic peaks at $114^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $185^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 2B), which was supporting the melting point and crystalline characters. However, it could be seen that three characteristic peaks of iodine disappeared entirely in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF, and $\operatorname{I}_{2}\textcircled{a}\gamma$ -CD in the range of $25\mathrm{-}300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which suggested that iodine was loaded into the carriers. $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF had a small endo­ thermic peak at $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , but the curve of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF was almost identical with KI-CD-MOF, which implied that the interaction between iodine and KI-CD-MOF is stronger than that with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF.
KI-CD-MOF and $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF had almost the same PXRD characteristics (Fig. 2C), which indicated their spatial structure is similar. The characteristic peak of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ disappeared in the pharmaceutical carrier, indicating that $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ successfully entered the cavity of the carrier and was evenly distributed in an amorphous state. The PXRD graph of the carrier after drug loading had changed to a small extent, but it still existed in an orderly structure state.
The effect of iodine on the porosity of CD-MOF and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ was studied by measuring the nitrogen adsorption capacity (Fig. 2D). The BET sur­ face area of KI-CD-MOF $(318.6\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ was lower than that of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}-$ CD-MOF $(424.5\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , which may be caused by varied structures of CDMOF and residual solvent. Drug molecules doping into CD-MOF was able to hinder the nitrogen uptake and led to the reduce of surface area, which could be used to confirm the interaction between CD-MOF and iodine. As a result, the BET surface areas of KI-CD-MOF and $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}.$ CD-MOF decreased to $0.91~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.54~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ after iodine loading, indicating that most of the pores of CD-MOF were occupied by iodine. However, there was no significant change in nitrogen adsorption for $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ because its pores were small.
The TGA thermograph showed the fast mass reduction in iodine samples from 30 to $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to sublimation of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ (Fig. 2E). The weight loss of KI-CD-MOF occurred at $25\mathrm{-}134^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $273-337\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , corre­ sponding to the loss of water and the cleavage of KI-CD-MOF, respec­ tively. The mass loss of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI-CD-MOF}$ occurred at $50{-}219^{\mathrm{~\circ~}}\mathrm{C}$ and $220{-}290^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which were caused by the loss of iodine and water in KI-CDMOF skeleton and the disruption of KI-CD-MOF skeleton, respectively. The changes in sublimation temperature of iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF indicated that there was an interaction between KI-CD-MOF and iodine. In addition, the weight loss rate of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF was significantly slower than that of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK-CD-MOF}$ within $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 2E, the inset), which indicated that $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF had stronger interactions between iodine and the carrier than $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF due to the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ and cyclodextrin molecular cavity encapsulation.
## 3.3. Drug loading efficiency and solubilization
It is observed that the cross-section of KOH-CD-MOF single-crystal became darker with time and reached saturation at about $15\ \mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 3A). This process further showed that the loading of iodine by KOH-CD-MOF was not surface adsorption but that iodine entered the cavity of KOH-CD-MOF.
The effects of the material ratio of iodine, drug loading time, and temperature on iodine loading efficiency were investigated. There was a positive correlation between drug loading and material ratio of iodine, and the loss of iodine decreased with the increase of the material ratio (Fig. 3B, C). The loss caused by using material ratios of iodine ( $30\%$ and $40\%$ ) was relatively similar, which was about $5\%$ . The temperature and time produced little effect on the drug loading of iodine (Fig. 3D, E). Hence, the optimal loading condition for $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF was achieved using a $30\%$ material ratio at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
SEM images (Fig. 3F) illustrated that the KI-CD-MOF was cubic and uneven particle size, distributed in the range of $3\mathrm{-}20~\upmu\mathrm{m}.$ and after the incorporation of iodine, the cubic shape and particle size range of KI-CDMOF particles were maintained. In addition, the morphology and par­ ticle size range of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ did not change after the iodine was incorporated. These illustrated that incorporation of iodine had no effect on the morphology and size of the carrier.
The poor solubility of iodine in water limits its application to some extent (Teng et al., 2021). The solubilities of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF, and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD in water were 3.86, 2.64, and 2.37 times as much that of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ $\mathrm{\cdot189.7~\upmug/mL}$ , respectively (Fig. 3G). It was considered that the solubilization effect of KI-CD-MOF is because of uniformly distributed $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ which combined with $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ to form $\bar{\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}}$ , and it can shift the dissolution equilibrium of iodine towards that dissolved in water.
## 3.4. Stability study
For stability study, the same material ratio $(30\%)$ was loaded by KICD-MOF, $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF, and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ . Because iodine was in a dy­ namic balance of binding and escaping while loading, the higher iodine loading of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF $(28.43\%)$ as compare to $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK-C}$ DMOF $(24.11\%)$ indicated that the capture of iodine by KI-CD-MOF was more stable than that by $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF. However, unadsorbed granular iodine that did not enter the cavity of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ could be observed on the surface of $\operatorname{I}_{2}@\gamma$ -CD (Fig. 4A), which did not occur on the surface of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK-CD-MOF}$ , indicating that the adsorbability of KI-CD-MOF and $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF was better than that of $\gamma$ -CD.
$\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF, and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD were exposed in a blast drying oven at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ , respectively. At $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 4B), the residual content of iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD decreased to $34.34\%$ at $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $4.27\%$ at $^{72\mathrm{~h},}$ , and that in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF decreased to $63.98\%$ at $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ . However, the residual content of iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF was $78.59\%$ at $72\mathrm{{h}}$ , which was almost $15\%$ higher than that in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF. These data illustrated that the stability of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI-CD-MOF}$ was significantly better than $\operatorname{I}_{2}@\gamma$ -CD and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . At $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 4C), the residual content of iodine in $\operatorname{I}_{2}\@parrow$ -CD decreased to $8.44\%$ at $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ and $2.41\%$ at $72\mathrm{{h}}.$ , and that in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF decreased to $51.23\%$ at $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . But the residual iodine content in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF was the highest at $72\mathrm{{h}}$ , about $82.28\%$ , which was 1.6 times and 34 times of the residual iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK-CD-MOF}$ and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD, respectively. It showed that the stability of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF was also remarkably better than $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upmu}$ -CD and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF at $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . At $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 4D), the residual content of iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD decreased to $3.95\%$ at $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ and $2.60\%$ at 72 h. The residual content of iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF was $84.22\%$ at $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , which was $14.46\%$ higher than that in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF $(69.76\%)$ , though the residual content of iodine in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}$ -CD-MOF and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF was almost identical $24\mathrm{h}$ later. These indicated that the stability of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF was better than $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK-CL}$ - MOF within $24\mathrm{h}$ . The results of 3 months accelerated stability test at the condition of $40\pm2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $75\%\pm5\%$ relative humidity showed that the residual content of iodine in $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF was still $92.5\%$ , while that in $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF was only $61.9\%$ (Fig. 4E), which indicated that $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}.$ -CD-MOF was more stable than $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD. Overall, the results indicated that the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ and cyclodextrin molecular cavity encapsulation in $\operatorname{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF makes the capture of iodine more stable than that of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK-CD}.$ - MOF and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\gamma}$ -CD.
## 3.5. Antibacterial activity
The antibacterial activities of ${\mathrm{I}}_{2},$ $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF, $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CDMOF, $\operatorname{I}_{2}@\gamma$ -CD, cydiodine and iodine tincture were tested on $E$ . coli CMCC (B) 44,102 and S. aureus CMCC (B) 26,112 strains by the deter­ mination of minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) and minimum bactericidal concentration (MBC) (Table 1). The MIC, MBC of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI-CD-}$
MOF and $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ were both $750~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ in $E$ . coli, indicating that the carrier had no effect on the antibacterial efficacy of iodine on E. coli, while the MIC, MBC of $\operatorname{I}_{2}\textcircled{a}\gamma$ -CD were $1500\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , suggesting that $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF had better antibacterial efficacy on $E_{\sun}$ . coli than $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD. In S. aureus, the MIC and MBC of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF were equal to those of ${\mathrm{I}}_{2},$ which were $93.75~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ and $750~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , respectively, indicating that the carrier did not influence the antibacterial efficacy of iodine on S. aureus. The MIC and MBC of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF were $187.5~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ and 1500 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , in addition, the MIC of iodine tincture was $375~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ , which were higher than those of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF. It meant that $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{K}$ I-CD-MOF had the best antibacterial efficacy on S. aureus compared with $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF and iodine tincture.
The time-kill profiles were evaluated at the concentration of $0.25\times$ MIC, $0.5\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , MIC, $2\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , $4\times\mathrm{MIC}$ , or higher concentration for 2, 4, 6 and $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . The control without treatment showed the exponential growth of bacteria. The time-kill curves showed a similar reduction pattern of E. coli. Population when treated with the samples within $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. 5A–C). When the concentration of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}_{2}@$ KI-CD-MOF was $188\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , a significant reduction in the bacterial count by 3 log cycles was observed within $2\mathrm{h}$ , but decreased by only one log cycle under the action of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF. The MIC value for each sample in S. aureus was significantly lower than that in E. coli. When the concentration of $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{KI}.$ - CD-MOF was 8 MIC $(750~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ , the bactericidal effect was observed within $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ of exposure time, while some $s.$ . aureus treated with $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF still survived within $^\mathrm{~8~h~}$ of exposure time (Fig. 5D–F). All these further illustrated that $\operatorname{I}_{2}({\underline{{a}}}\operatorname{KI}$ -CD-MOF had a better bacteriostatic effect on E. coli and S. aureus than $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOK}$ -CD-MOF, which was attributed to the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}$ and cyclodextrin molecular cavity encapsulation to achieve steady loading of iodine.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, KI-CD-MOF was synthesized to achieve high dispersion of $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ in the solid particulate carriers, which has been validated as an efficient carrier to capture and stabilize iodine. Iodine loading by gassolid reaction avoids the introduction of solvents and is ecologically green without pollution. The loading efficacy evaluations show that KICD-MOF is better than $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O K}$ -CD-MOF or $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ in improvement to the solubility and stability of iodine due to the synergistic effect of $\Gamma_{3}^{-}$ and cyclodextrin molecular cavity encapsulation. Meanwhile, antibacterial evaluation has further clarified its capability to enhance iodine for the antibacterial effects to E. coli and S. aureus, which helps to strengthen the advantage of iodine in the prevention and treatment of wound in­ fections. Overall, KI-CD-MOF has great potential as a new vector of iodine powder disinfectants.
